{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:c1","The Farey gap lemma","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. If </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>b</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>and</mtext></mstyle><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nthen <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></math></span>. The two\ncross-products <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></math></span> are positive integers, and </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> In\nparticular, a rational <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></math></span> strictly\nbetween the Farey neighbours <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span>, with positive denominator,\nhas denominator at least <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d1","An irrationality criterion from\nrational approximations","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></math></span> and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span> be in lowest\nterms, with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>x</mi></math></span> for every sufficiently large\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span> is irrational. Indeed, if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, a nonzero integer numerator gives\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span>, a\ncontradiction. This elementary criterion requires both nonvanishing and\nthe scaled error estimate. Convergence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi>x</mi></math></span> alone is insufficient, and the\napproximants need not be continued-fraction convergents.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d2","An irrationality criterion from near\nintegers","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Suppose that for every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> there are integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></math></span> with </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ξ</mi></math></span> is irrational: if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, every nonzero such difference is\nat least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span>. Restricting the\nmultiplier to powers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></math></span> of a\nfixed integer base <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> gives a\nsufficient special case, not a hypothesis satisfied by every irrational\nnumber. The strict lower bound excludes exact integer hits; the upper\nbound must be available for arbitrarily large <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>For a binary example, form a number by concatenating the blocks\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>10</mn></math></span> at square indices <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>01</mn></math></span> at the other indices. Its binary\nexpansion is not eventually periodic, since the block sequence has\nincreasingly long gaps between the square indices, so the number is\nirrational. There are no three consecutive equal digits. Every\nfractional part after a binary shift therefore lies in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span>, and the multiples <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>ξ</mi></math></span> do not approach the integers. This\nverifies that the restriction to base powers is genuinely stronger. This\nexample is an ordinary mathematical argument, not an additional claim\nabout the linked formalisation.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d3","A nonsingular evaluation matrix\ngives independence","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>I</mi></math></span> be\na finite index set and let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math></span>.\nIf there are evaluation points <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nthen the family <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> is\nlinearly independent over <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. A relation among the functions, evaluated at the <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></span>, gives a vector in the kernel of this\nnonsingular matrix, so all coefficients vanish. Finiteness is required\nto form the displayed determinant. In the totient application the\narithmetic work is to construct these evaluation points; the implication\nitself is ordinary linear algebra.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d9","The gap between distinct rational\nnumbers","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span> are\nreduced rational numbers with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The numerator <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></math></span> is a positive integer. Thus an\nupper bound on a positive rational error gives a lower bound on the\nproduct of the reduced denominators. A large denominator in an unreduced\ndisplayed expression need not be the reduced denominator appearing in\nthis conclusion.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a1a","The Möbius identity for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span>","<p><em> The absolutely convergent series\nsatisfy </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For the first\nrearrangement use <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span>. For the second use <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and the classical identity\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>.\nThese are identities of values, not transfers of irrationality from a\nLambert series with a different weight.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a1b","An equivalent irrationality question","<p><em> Subtracting the rational number <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> gives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This is\nthe same irrationality question in a different series representation.\nThe notation <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for the\nright-hand series is introduced in Definition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:a3\">6.29</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a2","The squared-Lambert identity","<p><em>\nLet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>w</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfy\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span> for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Expand <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></math></span> and\ngroup terms by <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></math></span>. Absolute\nconvergence follows from the bound on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>w</mi></math></span> and geometric decay. The cases <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi></math></span> give the next three series; the\nsame algebra applies to each, but their arithmetic values\ndiffer.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a4","Weight one and divisor sums","<p><em>\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo>′</mo></munderover><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo>′</mo></munderover><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. The displayed identity is\nformalised. Irrationality of its value follows from the cited linear\nindependence result of Postelmans and Van Assche, as explained in\nProposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:zetaq\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:zetaq\">5.17</a>; that\nliterature result is not formalised here. Replacing the weight <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>μ</mi></math></span> changes the value to that of\nDefinition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:a3\">6.29</a>.\nObservation <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:a6\">6.32</a>\ncompares the two choices of weight.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a5","Totient weight and gcd moments","<p><em> </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo>′</mo></munderover><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo>′</mo></munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi>Id</mi></mrow></math></span> (Pillai’s gcd-sum function). It also\nequals <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span> when\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></math></span> are independent and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></math></span> for\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>; see Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:pillai\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:pillai\">5.18</a>. This is a different\nweighted series. Its rationality is not settled by the identities proved\nhere.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a7","The weight of pairs divisible by a\nfixed integer","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></math></span> be\nindependent random variables with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></math></span> for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. For every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This follows by multiplying the two\ngeometric sums <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. It\nexplains the squared denominator in the preceding Möbius\nidentity.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a8","The sum over coprime directions","<p><em> The sum over positive coprime pairs satisfies </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> To see the\nnormalisation, write every pair of positive integers uniquely as <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and sum <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></math></span> over <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The total is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. This base-two\nnormalisation is used in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:a9a\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a9a\">6.35</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a9a","A Stern–Brocot recursion with\nstopping","<p><em> For positive integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span>, put </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nMultiplication by the common denominator proves the identity. At the\nroot <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, the three terms on the\nright are each <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>The first term is a mass retained at the current node, not passed\nto either child. Thus the probabilistic interpretation is a branching\nprocess with stopping: after division by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, the stopping and two transition\nprobabilities are, respectively, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> They sum to one. The stopping\nprobability is at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, which\ngives the next proposition’s geometric error bound. Also <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for the independent geometric variables used above. The\nidentity does not describe a conservative mass split between the two\nchildren alone.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a9b","Convergence with an explicit error","<p><em> In the splitting identity of Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:a9a\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:a9a\">6.35</a>, the sum of the\ntwo child terms is at most <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> of\nthe parent term. If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is the\nsum of the contributions removed during the first <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span> levels, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi></msup><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> In\nparticular, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.\nThis convergence statement is separate from identifying the limit with a\nsum over any independently defined infinite set of descendants; no\nadditional identification is asserted here.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b1","A numerator polynomial and its\ncoefficients","<p><em> For squarefree <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, the polynomial defined above has\ncoefficients </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing=\"1em\" rowspacing=\".2em\" columnalign=\"left left\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" symmetric=\"true\"></mo></mrow></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span>,\nextracting the coefficient gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span>; the usual\ndivisor formula for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi></math></span>\nevaluates this sum. All coefficients in the range <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span> are positive. Thus a signed\nsum of geometric polynomials has an explicit positive-coefficient\nexpression. The squarefree hypothesis is retained as in the cited formal\nstatements.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b2","Evaluation at two","<p><em> For\nsquarefree <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, evaluation of\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math></span> at two gives the integer </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Each quotient is\nan integer because <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span>. The\nformal definition sums over subsets of the prime divisors of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span>; for squarefree <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> these are exactly its divisors. This\nidentifies the polynomial calculation with the numerator used in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math></span>.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.json?v=4457e47d5986"}
